1
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:44,460
This video on ionizing radiation safety is to help you and your supervisor address radiation

2
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:46,540
safety in your section.

3
00:00:46,540 --> 00:00:52,460
It should be reviewed annually to satisfy initial and refresher radiation safety training.

4
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:57,220
After viewing this tape, please take a few minutes to discuss radiation safety procedures

5
00:00:57,220 --> 00:00:59,860
that pertain to your area.

6
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:04,980
With informed workers, responsible supervisors, and an aggressive safety program, we can reach

7
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:13,340
our goal of keeping all exposures to ionizing radiation a lara or as low as reasonably achievable.

8
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:18,580
Ionizing radiation is used throughout the Air Force in a multitude of applications.

9
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:24,160
Whether it's used to see inside a body or an aircraft part, ionizing radiation is high

10
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:29,420
energy and must be strictly controlled to prevent or reduce exposures.

11
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:35,020
This type of radiation emission includes high energy photons like x-rays and gamma rays

12
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:41,100
as well as particles like alpha, beta, and neutron radiation.

13
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:45,340
Ionizing radiation is just another property of matter in the form of energy release that

14
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:47,500
occurs naturally.

15
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:53,980
Whether it's coming from uranium or underground, rays from the sun or the cosmos, we are continually

16
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:59,100
throughout our lives being exposed to naturally occurring background radiation.

17
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:04,920
We can't control or limit background exposures, however, there is a lot we can do to control

18
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:09,160
and limit our occupational exposures.

19
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:14,380
Ionizing radiation can be generated by emitters that use large voltages to force electrons

20
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:17,700
to strike a target and release x-rays.

21
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:24,340
The x-rays generated are then filtered and collimated to produce x-rays of desirable strength.

22
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:29,780
Ionizing radiation is also produced by the natural decay of radioactive materials.

23
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:35,300
When radioactive materials decay, they revert to a more stable form and release energy in

24
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:38,460
the form of one or more of the following.

25
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:45,020
Gamma rays, alpha particles, beta particles, neutrons, light, and heat.

26
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:50,180
Different radioactive materials have different energy release signatures.

27
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:55,460
Radioactive materials are chosen on what type of energy release is required for the process.

28
00:02:55,460 --> 00:03:02,340
For example, a radioactive material called amorecium-241 is commonly used in smoke detectors

29
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:05,020
and in lantern pods.

30
00:03:05,020 --> 00:03:10,860
Amorecium-241 emits an alpha and gamma radiation when it decays, which meets the required

31
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:13,420
energy signature for the system.

32
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:19,440
It is important to note that materials that are irradiated do not normally become radioactive

33
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:25,720
unless they are subjected to extremely high radiation, as found in nuclear reactions.

34
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:30,880
Most materials that are exposed to ionizing radiation remain essentially unaffected.

35
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:36,760
However, ionizing radiation can and often does damage to cells that are exposed to high

36
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:38,720
enough levels.

37
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:43,800
This type of acute exposure is not likely to be significant unless large doses are

38
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:47,560
received, repeated exposures are experienced.

39
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:53,520
Large portions of the body are exposed and sensitive tissues are exposed.

40
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:57,360
Ionizing can change the DNA structure producing a mutation.

41
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:03,600
If the DNA is damaged at a certain spot and it can't repair itself, it may cause a tumor

42
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:04,600
or cancer.

43
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:10,720
When a DNA strand is mutated, the cell dies most of the time.

44
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:15,520
Repeated exposures increase the odds that one mutated cell will survive, producing a

45
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:17,480
cancerous growth.

46
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:22,880
Since more exposure equals more risk, all exposures must be minimized.

47
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:29,180
We can minimize our exposures through four main control measures, time, distance, shielding

48
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:33,000
and contamination control.

49
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,280
Time.

50
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:39,400
When working with radiation sources, the more time personnel are exposed to the source,

51
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:43,400
the more radiation exposure personnel will receive.

52
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:48,440
The exposure effects may be unnoticeable or negligible, but the overall dose is always

53
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:50,040
considered cumulative.

54
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:55,320
Therefore, careful steps must be taken to minimize exposure.

55
00:04:55,320 --> 00:05:00,240
Steps like rehearsing a radiation procedure, adjusting a source's power output to what

56
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:06,640
is needed, and careful management of on times will prevent unnecessary retakes and reduce

57
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:10,120
the time the source is used.

58
00:05:10,120 --> 00:05:11,600
Distance.

59
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:16,760
The most effective method of minimizing radiation exposures is to simply increase the

60
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:20,680
distance between the source and the personnel exposed.

61
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:26,160
The inverse square law applies to radiation emissions, in that the radiation intensities

62
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:31,000
fall off very rapidly as the distance increases.

63
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,440
For example, if the distance is doubled, the radiation intensity is reduced by a factor

64
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:37,440
of four.

65
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:41,440
Obviously, a few feet may make a difference.

66
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:47,120
Steps like keeping personnel as far away from the source as possible, establishing and maintaining

67
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:53,360
controlled areas, and directing beams away from potentially occupied areas are extremely

68
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:55,880
important.

69
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:57,580
Shielding.

70
00:05:57,580 --> 00:06:01,800
Any material placed between the source of the radiation and the potentially occupied

71
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:07,600
areas will absorb some of the radiation and thus reduce exposures.

72
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:12,680
In general, the more material used or the denser the material, the more radiation will

73
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:14,780
be absorbed.

74
00:06:14,780 --> 00:06:21,080
Shielding is generally required when areas adjacent to the radiation source are potentially occupied.

75
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:26,720
Good shielding begins with the proper design and installation of the shielding.

76
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:31,120
Careful consideration must be followed for renovations to ensure the shielding is not

77
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:32,920
compromised.

78
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:39,920
Shielding such as collimators must be used to restrict the beam to just the area of interest.

79
00:06:39,920 --> 00:06:44,600
Shielded facilities should be used whenever available or strict control measures must be

80
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:45,600
followed.

81
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:53,080
Last, simple shielding such as lead aprons must be used when required by the operation.

82
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:54,920
Contamination control.

83
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:58,960
This mainly applies to the use of radioactive materials.

84
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:04,120
When a radioactive source is suspected of being broken or potentially leaking, it should

85
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:11,360
be isolated and the radiation safety officer, or RSO, consulted immediately.

86
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:16,520
Proper hygiene is the easiest and most effective measure to prevent radioactive materials from

87
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:21,840
getting on the skin and close and to guard against accidental ingestion.

88
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:28,320
For all ionizing radiation operations, a radiation exposure assessment must be conducted

89
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:33,680
during initial performance of the operation and revalidated annually.

90
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:40,000
This survey is usually conducted by the base RSO, Bioenvironmental Engineer or Bioenvironmental

91
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:45,760
Engineering Technician, and involves a detailed review of the operation and a review of the

92
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:49,640
established or proposed safety measures.

93
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:54,000
Surveys are conducted using instruments such as an ionization chamber.

94
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:59,760
Ion chambers are enclosed volumes of air or some other gas with electrodes placed inside

95
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:03,120
to collect ions produced by radiation.

96
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:07,400
By measuring the current, you can measure the relative radiation rate.

97
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:15,000
Geiger-Mueller or GM tubes like those commonly found in the ADM 300A Radiac set can also

98
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,600
be used for radiation detection.

99
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:23,480
Radiation measurements are actually energy rates and are usually displayed in micro or

100
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:26,320
milli-renkins per hour.

101
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:32,360
Measurements are taken where all potential exposures may occur, including any possible public areas.

102
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:38,200
The RSO or qualified technician then uses the measured radiation rates and reported usage

103
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:43,840
rates to determine the maximum worst-case exposure a worker or a member of the public

104
00:08:43,840 --> 00:08:45,800
could be exposed to.

105
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:51,720
In strict accordance with Title X, Code of Federal Regulations Part 20, Standards of

106
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:58,440
Protection Against Radiation, workers may not be exposed to more than 5 REMs or Renkin's

107
00:08:58,440 --> 00:09:02,240
equivalent MAN in a calendar year.

108
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:06,840
Pregnant workers more than 0.5 REM during their pregnancy, and the public may not be

109
00:09:06,840 --> 00:09:09,800
exposed to more than 0.1 REM per year.

110
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:16,680
If the RSO determines that excessive exposure exists, engineering controls, personal protective

111
00:09:16,680 --> 00:09:23,440
equipment, and administrative controls to reduce the exposure potential may be recommended.

112
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:28,360
Based on the exposure potential, the RSO may decide to place personnel on a monthly or

113
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:31,280
quarterly dissimetry program.

114
00:09:31,280 --> 00:09:36,840
Certain jobs require personnel to be automatically placed in a dissimetry program.

115
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:41,640
Pregnant radiation workers are required to be entered into monthly dissimetry.

116
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:47,240
For these personnel, the Base Bio-Environmental Engineering Office will assign Thermo-Luminescent

117
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:54,280
Dissimeters, or TLDs, to each individual and collect them on a monthly or quarterly basis.

118
00:09:54,280 --> 00:10:00,400
TLDs operate very simply by absorbing the radiation they are exposed to, and later release

119
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:04,560
that information when heated in a TLD reading instrument.

120
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:08,800
By measuring the amount of energy released, comparing it to the amount released by the

121
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:14,040
control badge, and running the information through a computer program, the lab can determine

122
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:16,480
an individual's exposure.

123
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:22,000
The results are sent out either monthly or quarterly on these computer-generated reports.

124
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:30,960
Armstrong listing AL 1499-1 and 1499-2 for each individual.

125
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:36,280
The report details the individual's exposure for the last monitoring period and for the

126
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:38,600
year-to-date exposures.

127
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:45,520
The report also breaks out the exposures to the extremities and specific target organs.

128
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:52,720
At the end of each year, an AF Form 1527 is sent to document the individual's exposure.

129
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:58,120
After the form is signed by the RSO, it is sent to the individual to review.

130
00:10:58,120 --> 00:11:03,760
There are several types of badges used depending on the type of operation being performed.

131
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:08,320
A whole-body dissimeter badge is the most common and is always required no matter what

132
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:11,160
other type of dissimeter is worn.

133
00:11:11,160 --> 00:11:15,720
The whole-body dissimeter must be clipped on the front of the body, below the shoulders

134
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:22,120
and above the hips, and is always worn underneath any personal protective equipment in use, especially

135
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:23,880
lead aprons.

136
00:11:23,880 --> 00:11:28,480
The collar dissimeter is worn on the collar and is required to determine exposures to the

137
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:33,000
head and eyes when a lead apron covers the whole-body dissimeter.

138
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:39,040
A neutron dissimeter is worn flat against the body's midsection, usually with a belt.

139
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:44,840
A ring dissimeter is worn to determine radiation exposures to the hand and forearm, primarily

140
00:11:44,840 --> 00:11:51,600
in conjunction with fluoroscopy or for personnel who regularly handle radioactive materials.

141
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:55,640
A dissimeter must never leave the work area and should be stored with the area control

142
00:11:55,640 --> 00:11:58,520
badge when not in use.

143
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:03,480
Personnel should never allow the TLD to be exposed to heat and sunlight as this may affect

144
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:05,000
the readings.

145
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:09,640
For personal monitoring, digital alarm dissimeters or DADS are used.

146
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:14,640
These monitors have small ion chambers incorporated into them.

147
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:20,560
They are usually required for personnel who work around high-radiation areas.

148
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:25,480
A digital alarm dissimeter should be used in conjunction with a TLD but never in lieu

149
00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:27,400
of a TLD.

150
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:34,120
It is an important reminder that DADS and TLDs offer no protection for radiation exposures.

151
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:39,080
All the required procedures for maximizing the effectiveness of time, distance, shielding

152
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:45,320
and contamination control should be spelled out in a unit-specific radiation safety operating

153
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:47,280
instruction.

154
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:53,040
This OI should be concise but cover shop-specific operations, training requirements, protective

155
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:58,560
equipment required, engineering controls and procedures of what to do if you suspect an

156
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,560
overexposure.

157
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:07,000
If during an operation you identify a potential problem, you must do the minimum, cease operations

158
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:14,080
and secure the area, seek medical assistance if required, contact your supervisor, contact

159
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:16,520
the base RSO.

160
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:21,320
For after-duty hour emergencies, the RSO can usually be contacted through the command

161
00:13:21,320 --> 00:13:26,000
post or through the after-hours medical care phone line.

162
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:31,480
Have each individual complete a statement of events including any and all operating parameters,

163
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:36,000
distances, observations, times and conditions.

164
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:41,120
Will ionizing radiation hurt my chances for having children?

165
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:46,800
Ionizing radiation can affect the DNA of a chromosome as previously discussed.

166
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:51,680
Because most mutations are recessive, chance mutation would only present themselves if

167
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:57,000
the genes from both parents have the same mutation at the same location on a given pair

168
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:58,720
of chromosomes.

169
00:13:58,720 --> 00:14:03,560
However unlikely, the effect is cumulative for females as the eggs are developed at

170
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:06,320
birth and never replace themselves.

171
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:10,360
It is another reason to maintain exposures a lara.

172
00:14:10,360 --> 00:14:13,960
Will ionizing radiation affect my pregnancy?

173
00:14:13,960 --> 00:14:19,120
A developing fetus is highly susceptible to ionizing radiation, especially in the first

174
00:14:19,120 --> 00:14:21,080
trimester.

175
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:25,240
It is critical that women who suspect that they may be pregnant get a blood test as soon

176
00:14:25,240 --> 00:14:30,760
as possible in order to be placed in the Air Force Fetal Protection Program.

177
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:35,820
The program requires the workplace to be evaluated for possible factors that may adversely

178
00:14:35,820 --> 00:14:37,680
affect pregnancy.

179
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:43,940
Based on the workplace factors, work restrictions or modifications may be required.

180
00:14:43,940 --> 00:14:54,620
Women who require an x-ray are asked and often tested to ensure they are not pregnant.

181
00:14:54,620 --> 00:14:58,860
An overexposure is difficult to detect without instrumentation.

182
00:14:58,860 --> 00:15:03,460
Most people suspect they are exposed only after they realize a mistake may have been

183
00:15:03,460 --> 00:15:04,460
made.

184
00:15:04,460 --> 00:15:10,940
A unit left open, a safety device defeated, inadequate area signs and such.

185
00:15:10,940 --> 00:15:19,740
All suspected overexposures must be reported and then investigated.

186
00:15:19,740 --> 00:15:25,000
Your supervisor, base RSO, or Bioenvironmental Engineering.

187
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:31,140
They have workplace information and documentation about the radiation exposure potential.

188
00:15:31,140 --> 00:15:36,060
Your base should have a specific instruction about radiation and a lara.

189
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:44,100
For radioactive materials, AFI 40-201, managing radioactive materials in the U.S. Air Force

190
00:15:44,100 --> 00:15:49,460
gives excellent guidance on how we manage our radioactive materials.

191
00:15:49,460 --> 00:15:55,180
Some operations that require radioactive materials have specific technical orders governing proper

192
00:15:55,180 --> 00:15:57,300
usage and handling.

193
00:15:57,300 --> 00:16:01,780
Others are restrictive materials and require a radioactive material permit.

194
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:10,180
Finally, the governing regulation for all radiation exposures is 10 CFR 20.

195
00:16:10,180 --> 00:16:15,140
In closing, ionizing radiation is a powerful tool but a potential health hazard.

196
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:19,340
We need not be fearful of it, but we must respect it.

197
00:16:19,340 --> 00:16:25,220
With informed workers, responsible supervisors and an aggressive safety program, we can keep

198
00:16:25,220 --> 00:16:29,900
all exposures to ionizing radiation as low as reasonably achievable.
